a twin attack at night with drones on  two major Saudi oil facilities one of  largest in the world this was an  extraordinary operation the scale the  method and the impact for the Yemeni  rebel army who carried it out it was so  successful that it has reportedly forced  Saudi Arabia to shut down half its oil  production the two plants were hit just  before 4:00 a.m. and they were burning  into the morning they are east of the  capital Riyadh hundreds of miles from  Yemen and yet it seems still an  achievable target for the Iranian backed  rebel group analysts have long said that  this plant is Saudi Arabia's Achilles  heel if it is shut down for an extended  period the implications will be global  [Music]  on a TV station owned by the Houthi  rebels a commander boasted of a precise  attack with ten drones  he claimed that help from people inside  Saudi Arabia had made this possible and  he said it was a measured response to  crimes and aggression from Saudi Arabia  and her allies inside Yemen for four and  a half years now a saudi-led coalition  with British weapons has been fighting  alongside the government in Yemen  against the Houthi rebel group whose  back as Iran make this a dangerous power  struggle the consequence is regional  instability and the world's worst  humanitarian crisis an intractable mess  with no clear Victor the Hootie's now  feel as though there is great  opportunity to bring this first of all  to the global awareness and they  certainly have chosen a major location  to do that the overnight attack follows  a pattern in June a Houthi cruise  missile struck an international airport  in March last year seven ballistic  missiles were fired some reaching the  Saudi capital Riyadh one person was  killed and in December 2017 Saudi air  defenses intercepted a Houthi missile  which had a royal palace in its sights  but the use of small drones here is a  development to achieve new technologies  low-grade attacks are disproportionately  effective and are becoming a global  threat  two weeks ago Israel released this  footage  which acclaim shows iranian-backed  fighters in syria trying to launch an  attack drone to hit Israel they claimed  Iran's elite hoods force was behind it  there is no proof that Iran was behind  last night's attack but the Kurds Force  Commander Qasem Soleimani praised the  Houthi tactics the Saudi government says  the fires are now under control but the  damage physically isn't clear and  economically it could be huge mark stone  Sky News
 

